/* book Worked example 2: Q: The wing of the first example (semi-span 8 m, net distributed load 9 kN/m upward) also carries an engine 3 m from the root. At the design load factor the engine’s inertia load is 40 kN downward (an engine of about 1630 kg at n = 2.5). Find the shear force and bending moment at the root and on each side of the engine, and the largest shear on the span.
   1. Outboard of the engine (3 m < x ≤ 8 m) nothing changes: V = 9(8 − x) and M = 9(8 − x)²/2.
   2. Just outboard of the engine, x = 3 m: V = 9 × 5 = 45 kN; M = 9 × 5² ÷ 2 = 112.5 kN·m.
   3. Crossing the engine, the outboard load drops by 40 kN: just inboard of it, V = 45 − 40 = 5 kN. M does not jump: it is still 112.5 kN·m at x = 3 m.
   4. Root shear: V = 9 × 8 − 40 = 72 − 40 = 32 kN.
   5. Root moment: M = 9 × 8² ÷ 2 − 40 × 3 = 288 − 120 = 168 kN·m.
   6. Largest shear: 45 kN, just outboard of the engine, which is more than the 32 kN at the root.
   Answer: Root shear 32 kN and root moment 168 kN·m; at the engine the shear jumps from 45 kN (outboard) to 5 kN (inboard) while M stays 112.5 kN·m. The largest shear, 45 kN, is at the engine station, not the root. */
