/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Cabin pressure p is a differential pressure: inside absolute pressure minus outside ambient pressure.
   - σh is the hoop stress round the circumference and σl the longitudinal stress along the axis, both in Pa; p is in Pa; r and t are in m.
   - Hoop stress is twice the longitudinal stress, so a fatigue crack in plain skin tends to run along the fuselage.
   - Treat the fuselage as a beam held at the wing.
   - The tail load comes from pitch balance.
   - W is the weight in N; a and lt are in m; M₀ is in N·m.
   - This is how the V-n diagram sizes the tail and fuselage: each corner gives n and a speed; the speed sets M₀, which grows with dynamic pressure; the formula gives P.
   - The fin carries a side load F from rudder or sideslip at a height h above the fuselage axis.
   - Hoop and longitudinal stresses act on perpendicular faces, so they are not simply added.
   - σvm is the equivalent stress in Pa; + applies on the tension side of the bend, − on the compression side.
*/
