/* book Worked example: Q: A fuselage has mean radius 1.2 m and skin 1.6 mm thick. Cabin differential pressure is 55 kPa and the ultimate bending moment at a section is 200 kN·m. The skin is 2024-T3 (yield about 345 MPa, ultimate about 485 MPa). Treat the section as a plain skin tube. Find σh, σl, I, σb, the total σx on each side of the bend and the von Mises stress, and comment on the margin.
   1. σh = p r / t = 55 000 × 1.2 ÷ 0.0016 = 41.25 MPa. σl = p r / (2t) = 20.63 MPa.
   2. I ≈ π r³ t = π × 1.728 × 0.0016 = 8.686 × 10⁻³ m⁴.
   3. σb = (200 000 × 1.2) ÷ (8.686 × 10⁻³) = 27.63 MPa at y = r.
   4. Tension side: σx = 20.63 + 27.63 = 48.26 MPa. Compression side: σx = 20.63 − 27.63 = −7.01 MPa.
   5. σvm = √(48.26² − 48.26 × 41.25 + 41.25²) = √2039.6 = 45.16 MPa. The compression side gives the same 45.16 MPa, because the two values of σx sit equally either side of σh/2.
   6. Margin: 45.2 MPa at ultimate load is far below 485 MPa; at limit load (÷ 1.5) it is about 30 MPa, far below the 345 MPa yield. The −7.0 MPa side still needs a buckling check, and pressure fatigue is checked separately.
   Answer: σh = 41.25 MPa, σl = 20.63 MPa, I = 8.686 × 10⁻³ m⁴, σb = 27.63 MPa, σx = 48.26 and −7.01 MPa, σvm = 45.2 MPa on both sides. Static strength passes easily; buckling and fatigue decide the design. */
