/* book exam traps: Adding hoop stress straight into the longitudinal total. They act at right angles; combine them with von Mises. | Drawing the rear-fuselage moment largest at the tail. It is zero at the tail and grows forward to the wing; only the tailplane’s own moment peaks at its root. | Forgetting the tail mass’s inertia: at n = 2.5 an 800 kg tail adds 19.6 kN to the download. | Using absolute cabin pressure instead of differential pressure. At 11 km the outside pressure is about 23 kPa, so a cabin held near 75 kPa absolute is loaded by about 52 kPa. | Using π r⁴/4 for a thin tube. The thin-tube value is I ≈ π r³ t. */
